    if r_state >= START+8: then r_state <= HOTIDLE; r_idle=1; o_stb=1; o_byte <= r_ireg; o_sclk=1; else r_state <= r_state+1 (duplicate? Actually line 264: r_state <= r_state+1; but line 254 already did r_state <= r_state+1. So there is a duplicate increment. Let's see: line 254: r_state <= r_state + 1; line 256-264: if (r_state >= LLSDSPI_START+8) begin ... end else r_state <= r_state + 1; So if condition false, r_state gets incremented twice! That's a bug. Because line 254 increments, then line 264 increments again. So for states START to START+7, r_state increments by 2 per cycle? But note: the condition "else if (o_sclk)" is only entered when o_sclk is 1. In that block, o_sclk is set to 0 (line 255). Then next cycle, o_sclk is 0, so the "else begin" (line 265) executes: r_ireg <= {r_ireg[6:0], i_miso}; o_sclk <= 1;. Then next cycle o_sclk is 1 again, so the "else if (o_sclk)" block executes again. So each bit takes two cycles: one with o_sclk=1 (shift out MOSI, increment state) and one with o_sclk=0 (shift in MISO, set o_sclk=1). The state should increment only once per bit (i.e., per two cycles). But the code increments r_state in the o_sclk=1 phase (line 254) and then again in the else clause (line 264) if not at the end. That would cause the state to increment twice per bit, so after 8 bits, the state would go from START to START+16? But there is a check at line 256: if (r_state >= LLSDSPI_START+8). If r_state increments by 2 each time, then after 4 bits, r_state would be START+8? Let's trace:

